
If you do a lot of these derivative of polynomial problems, eventually
you will see why the following important things are true:

1. Differentiation (i.e. taking the derivative) is a linear operation.
That is, the derivative of a sum (or difference) is the sum (or
difference) of the individual derivatives. Note: The derivative of y(x)
is often written y'(x).

If y(x) = f(x) + g(x) then y'(x), the derivative of y(x), is f'(x) +
g'(x).

2. Constants "float thru the differentiation process".

If y(x)= cf(x), where c is some constant, then y'(x) = cf'(x).

3. The "power rule"

If y = x^n, where n is any integer, then y'(x) = nx^(n-1). Bring down
the exponent as a multiplier and lower the exponent by 1. Very important
rule. Should memorize.

Example of all the above using Problem 7b

y(x) = 2x^2 - 10x

y'(x) = 2(2x^(2-1)) - 10(1)x^(1-1) = 4x - 10x^0 = 4x - 10

